  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute gout of the right big toe, a first flare, drug-induced (M10.271)</content>. A typical first gout flare of the big toe (podagra), with sudden overnight onset, redness, swelling and maximal tenderness within a day, in a man taking a thiazide diuretic, which raises serum urate. No fever, no skin break, and a single typical joint make septic arthritis unlikely, so the joint was not aspirated; he will return at once if he develops fever or the joint worsens. Serum urate can be falsely normal during a flare, so it will be measured once the flare has settled. Low-dose colchicine is chosen: an oral NSAID would add to the bleeding risk of aspirin and raise blood pressure that is not yet at target. Atorvastatin with colchicine slightly raises the risk of muscle toxicity at higher colchicine doses; the short low-dose course is acceptable with a warning about muscle pain. [S]<list><item>Colchicine 1.2 mg now and 0.6 mg an hour later, then 0.6 mg twice a day until the flare settles, for up to a week; stop if diarrhea develops.</item><item>Rest and elevate the foot, and ice for 20 minutes several times a day.</item><item>Acetaminophen up to 1 g three times a day if needed.</item><item>Report muscle pain or weakness at once.</item><item>Return at once for fever, spreading redness, or another hot joint.</item><item>Review the diuretic at the twelve-week visit, with a serum urate.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Stage 2 hypertension (I10)</content>. 142/86 today, in pain; continue the current medicines until the review. [S]<list><item>Continue hydrochlorothiazide and amlodipine for now.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2020 American College of Rheumatology Guideline for the Management of Gout (FitzGerald JD, et al. Arthritis Care Res 2020;72(6):744-760) (2020)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Telephone check in three days. Twelve-week review in three weeks, with a serum urate. Next scheduled visit 1930-09-29: twelve-week review: blood pressure and lipids after a gout flare on hydrochlorothiazide; switch to losartan. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
